Confirmed: `eraseRange` (what `EL`/`\x1b[K` calls) sets cell content to 0 — those cells vanish from `getText()` output (codePoint 0 skipped). So:

- A row whose trailing cells were explicitly WRITTEN as spaces → `getText()` returns trailing spaces (padded).
- A row whose trailing cells were EL-erased or never written → `getText()` ends at last written char (unpadded).

And the dart_tui renderer has TWO paint paths with DISAGREEING tail behavior:
- `_paintRow` (scroll fast path) ends each painted row with `\x1b[K` → row tails ERASED → unpadded.
- `_diffAndEmit` (cell-diff path) writes only changed cells and NEVER erases the row tail → whatever cells were materialized on that physical line before REMAIN (stale written spaces from an earlier full repaint stay) → padded.

Which path a given frame takes depends on `_detectScrollShift` heuristics (tolerance budgets, static-edge invariant, mismatch counts) — i.e., on exact frame contents and timing. On macOS/fa-m5 (slower, different frame interleavings — see the mid frame in the log: the settle notice interleaved differently than linux) the `· older` row ended up on a physical line holding stale written-space cells → padded `before` vs unpadded `mid` → raw `List<String>` equality fails even though the row's CONTENT (the frozen counts) is byte-identical.

